Don't really know what to say :) I've been away from all Roguelikes for some
years (it comes and goes - when the urge hits, its usually pretty powerful).
I came back and started playing Adom a few weeks ago - wound up beating it
for the first time. After that, I figured why not give Nethack a shot. After
a few abortive first efforts (playing random characters), Chumpy the Valk
was born, and she just never stopped.

I only had one major mishap during the game, when Mjollnir mysteriously
vanished in Fort Ludios. I had never been to the fort before, but I didn't
notice any special properties that could account for my prized hammer
vanishing :( I reverted to my backup weapon, Grayswandir, and used that for
the remainder of the game.

I wound up getting my ac very low (sub -40) for the first time ever, and
heavily utilized magic markers and recharging for once, which was also a
first. Gehennom was still a horrible, horrible boring slog, but I made it
through and retrieved the Amulet. Curiously, I've had characters in the past
make it this far as well, but I always wound up quitting before I ascended
to the top again - why I'm not exactly sure. In any event, this time I was
undeterred, and dutifully made my way back up to the top. I had prepared
well by hacking out passages between all of the staircases, and this sped my
ascent considerably.

Once I reached the top, I had some nervous moments in the Planes. I thought
I had prepared well, but with no prior experience, I didn't know for sure.
The first real 'oh no' moment was on the Plane of Fire, when I finally
located the portal and it wouldn't work. I realized why - I was missing the
Amulet. Apparently the Wizard had stolen it, and I didn't notice, as I was
doing levitating sweeps back and forth across the level to locate the
portal. I probably had killed him over lava. Despondent, I went back to the
last place where I had fought him, and used up all means of cold projection
I possessed, then dug up the squares. My loot consisted of two crossbow
bolts :/ I also managed to die (twice!) here, getting stuck in the lava.
Both times, I was saved by AoLS (I had four!). Is there any way to escape
lava once you get stuck? I tried putting on a Ring of Levitation, but that
didn't work, nor did Teleportation. The second time was just a stupid
misstep, but the first was due to lava pouring in on me from an adjacent
square when I tried to dig down (learned that one the hard way, thank
goodness it wasn't permanently fatal).

Earth and Air were relatively quick, but Water was an absolute nightmare. I
spent forever in bubbles, fighting off the Wizard, killing monsters, moving
around, searching, switching bubbles, accidentally fell in twice (no harm
done apparently, greased Bag of Holding seemed ok). I finally accidentally
discovered that Levitation works! This contorted my brain a bit, but I soon
found the Portal.

Once I reached the Astral Plane, I was staggered by the number of monsters
present (Orb of Fate's warning is *cool*!). I considered my inventory and
decided to try on a Ring of Conflict... I'm not entirely sure if this was
wise or not. The peaceful companion my god had granted me not only became
hostile, he summoned more to attack me! The whole level then erupted in
chaos. The various Riders weren't *too* bad, but I was very nervous around
Famine, as the long Fire and Water delays had sapped my food supplies.
Fortunately they weren't super tough, so Famine (and the others) died fairly
quickly. Pestilence did give me some scares also, when he kept sickening me,
and I had to repeatedly hammer on the unicorn horn without a chance to hit
him back, but he eventually died as well. I managed to make and fight my way
to both wrong altars before finally reaching the altar of Tyr and offering
the Amulet successfully :)

Phew. Only took me, oh, 14? years or so to beat this game :) On and off
obviously (mostly off), but I've been playing roguelikes now and then ever
since I discovered computers when I was young.

Special thanks to Boudewijn for pointing me to the story of Krysia's
Krusader. That amazing story was inspiration enough for me to pick up
Nethack again and give it a shot. And what do you know, it worked!

